description = "M28AI companion, campaign only. Enables M28AI's combined AI/human armies (per-unit, manual takeover of your own units) in single-player campaigns, where the game rebuilds ScenarioInfo.Options and drops all lobby game options. Also enforces AI-inherit at the engine event level so units built by a taken-over factory/engineer really do inherit the takeover (M28AI's own propagation is unreliable on the retail build). Units are never taken over until you toggle them. v9 fixes a takeover that switched itself off when a factory finished building: player intent now comes only from the toggle click, never inferred from unit state, and a missing M28CombinedArmy option is restored automatically if the campaign rebuilds its Options table. v10 adds experimental fire spreading: a group of taken-over experimentals no longer all shells the same enemy, extra units shift to nearby less-contested targets (ACU/SACU/enemy experimentals/T3 structures stay exempt so priority kills get full weight), and a locked target now holds for 8 seconds (12 for experimentals) unless a high-value target appears. v11 cuts reconcile load: only your own units are tracked (enemy AI units were being tracked and their UI stats rewritten for nothing, thousands of C calls a second), the built/completed check is cached per unit, and the reconcile thread now stops immediately when the last tracked unit disappears instead of idling for 20 more seconds into the end-of-mission cleanup. v12 hardens the sweep against the end-of-mission teardown that crashed two sessions: it stands down for 20 seconds when more than half of your tracked units vanish inside one pass, never calls GetStat/UpdateStat on a unit whose Dead flag is set, and re-checks each unit once its brain attaches so enemy units no longer pad the registry. It also fixes a target pin that never expired (the timer was renewed even on the passes where the wrapper had just overridden the choice, so a unit could stay locked on one enemy forever), and wraps the three behaviour hooks in pcall so an error can never kill M28AI's command thread. v8's aggression tuning is still in: lower enemy-threat valuation, no dodge/hover micro, original fast order cycle, no retreat micro for your land combat units, and a reroll when a factory would repeat its last pick. M28AI keeps full control of its own housekeeping, including destroying surplus units; no unit or factory caps are applied, and M28AI itself is never modified. Requires M28AI v328 enabled; harmless no-op otherwise. Skirmish and multiplayer behaviour is never touched. v13 keeps the takeover button honest: its tick state used to be computed once when you selected a unit and then never updated, and a click inverted whatever the screen happened to show, so the same click could turn a unit on or off depending on how stale the icon was. The button is now refreshed about ten times a second from the authoritative state, a click sends the state the unit should end up in, and units with no builder (ACU, mission-spawned) have their UI state mirrored from M28AI's own field instead of drifting from it. v15 replaces that approach: reading the M28Active stat back proved unreliable in campaign (it is not a blueprint General.StatToggles stat, so the engine does not sync it to the UI the way it syncs engine stats, and re-selecting a unit read 0 again no matter what had been clicked). The tick now comes from a click memory kept on the UI side - what you clicked, for that unit id - with the stat, then the builder's click memory, as fallbacks, and a failed read leaves the button alone instead of forcing it dark. The per-frame refresh is gone, so no refresh can undo a click. v15 also fixes the reason the sim-side projection never ran: units were dropped at creation because their brain was still the temporary one, so the registry was always empty (slots=0); registration and identification are now separate, with a short grace period before a non-player unit is dropped. v14 fixes the regression that came with v13: the per-frame refresh read the M28Active stat, which travels to the sim and back over a network round trip, so it overwrote the freshly painted tick with the pre-click value within 0.1 seconds and the button looked unresponsive. A click now paints the target state and holds it until the stat actually catches up (or two seconds elapse, which logs a warning), and a second click in that window inverts the held target rather than the stale stat."
